     2,
     "On 14-03-08 02:09 PM, Eduardo Bologna wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi all, I&#39;m starting with ggplot and I can&#39;t solve this (apparently\r<br>&gt; simple) problem. I&#39;m using a sample of survey data (eph100) where P47T\r<br>&gt; is individual income. The syntax\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  qplot(P47T, data = eph100, colour=&quot;Red&quot;, binwidth=500)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  puts red borders, but\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  qplot(P47T, data = eph100, colour=&quot;Blue&quot;, binwidth=500)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  does the same: red borders.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "  You need to protect explicit values (rather than mappings) with I()\r<br>when using qplot, e.g.\r<br>\r<br> qplot(P47T, data = eph100, colour=I(&quot;Red&quot;), binwidth=500)\r<br>\r<br> and similarly for fill etc.\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
